This paper presents an innovative cyber threat exchange framework grounded in decentralized training, enabling the sharing of anonymized traffic data among distinct entities via a danger exchange system (DES). As traffic data submission and exchange face two primary hurdles - maintaining confidentiality and verifying the trustworthiness of partners - the proposed framework seeks to overcome these obstacles. Firstly, it ensures traffic data confidentiality is preserved through the use of two advanced methodologies: a federated learning technique and a differential learning approach. Secondly, it establishes a method for establishing trust among partners by evaluating and confirming the accuracy of exchanged models during training. A demonstration based on a study involving actual traffic data highlights the efficacy of the proposed framework in improving breach detection precision when compared to traditional methods. Lastly, this research investigates the potential of the cyber threat exchange framework to grow and adapt in an ever-changing 'adversary landscape'. Experimental results show that this framework can adapt to unseen breaches within three iterations. Further details about this research can be found at https://github.com/saleh2017/cyber-threat-exchange. Ultimately, this study aims to establish the cyber threat exchange framework as a viable solution for organizations to collaborate in detecting threats and enhancing their security without compromising confidentiality or integrity. As such, this research may serve as a model for analogous problems within the broader domain of internet security. Key words: Federated Learning, Differential Learning, Privacy, Trust Establishment, Cyber Threat Sharing, Network Breach Detection, Traffic Anonymization. 

